@*
    One seat: who is in it, what it has banked, its discard pile, the cards lying face up in
    front of it, and whether the table is waiting on it. Nothing else about its hand — a hand
    is concealed until a declaration (RULES.md §7.1), and a watcher is not sent one at all
    (TableFanOut). The two exceptions are the rules' own (P41): every card of every pile may be
    looked through (§5), and a card taken in the open lies face up in front of its taker for as
    long as it stays in their hand (§5.2).

    🔥 Compact on purpose. A player panel carries identity, money and turn order and nothing
    else, so that the felt can be big — Board Game Arena's rule, and the reason there is no
    prose in here at all. Every marker below is a glyph for the eye with a word beside it for
    the ear (§3.11 A2), which is how the panel says four things in the space of one sentence.

    ⚠️ The ▶ means "it is their turn", and it only started meaning that in P13.5. Before the
    server broadcast TurnBegan the nearest the public game had was "moved last", and a glyph
    pointing at the seat that had just finished is worse than no glyph at all.
*@

<li class="seat @Place @(Seat.IsTheirTurn ? "turn" : null) @(Yours ? "yours" : null) @(Seat.PlayedByTheComputer ? "away" : null)">
    <p class="who">
        <span class="marker" aria-hidden="true">@Marker</span>
        @*
            ⚠️ The name is allowed to be cut short — a person may type twenty-four characters and
            a computer seat is named for how it plays since P19 (Aung Aung (medium)) — so the
            whole of it is on the element for a hover and for anything reading the page. The
            visible text is still the accessible name, so this adds a tooltip and takes nothing
            away (§3.11 A).
        *@
        <span class="name" title="@Seat.Name">@Seat.Name</span>
        <span class="said">@State(Seat)</span>
    </p>

    @*
        ✅ §3.11 C16 — a dropped circuit must not look like a dropped game. The event has been
        broadcast since P13.2 and the log has said it since P13.3; P13.6 says it in the seat,
        which is where somebody looking for their own name will look.
    *@
    @if (Seat.PlayedByTheComputer)
    {
        <p class="away">
            <span class="marker" aria-hidden="true">@StoodIn</span>
            <span>Played by the computer</span>
        </p>
    }

    <p class="bank">
        <span class="amount @Direction" aria-hidden="true">@Amount</span>
        <span class="said">banked @Amount</span>
    </p>

    @*
        🔥 The cards this seat holds face up (RULES.md §5.2, P41): taken in the open, part of
        their concealed hand, and visible to everybody for as long as they hold them. Drawn
        flat rather than behind a control — a rule that makes something visible is not honoured
        by a disclosure you have to open.
    *@
    @if (Seat.FaceUp.Count > 0)
    {
        <p class="faceup">
            <span class="marker" aria-hidden="true">@FaceUpMark</span>
            <span class="said">Face up in front of them, still in their hand:</span>
            @foreach (var held in Seat.FaceUp)
            {
                <CardChip @key="held.Id.Value" Card="held" TokenWords="face up in their hand" />
            }
        </p>
    }

    <div class="discard">
        @if (Seat.LastDiscard is { } card)
        {
            @*
                ⚠️ The whole pile, on demand (RULES.md §5): every card of every pile may be
                looked through, and until P41 no front end let a player do it. The top card
                stays on the summary — it is the one the seat after them may take — and
                opening the disclosure is the looking-through. A real control (§3.11 A4).
            *@
            <details class="pile">
                <summary>
                    <CardChip Card="card" Token="@Threw" TokenWords="on top of their discard pile" />
                    <span class="count" aria-hidden="true">@Seat.Pile.Count</span>
                    <span class="said">Look through their pile of @Seat.Pile.Count.</span>
                </summary>
                <ol class="thrown" aria-label="Their discard pile, oldest first">
                    @foreach (var thrown in Seat.Pile)
                    {
                        <li @key="thrown.Id.Value"><CardChip Card="thrown" /></li>
                    }
                </ol>
            </details>
        }
        else
        {
            <span class="empty" aria-hidden="true">@Threw</span>
            <span class="said">Nothing on their discard pile yet.</span>
        }
    </div>
</li>

@code {
    /// <summary>What the discard pile is marked with when it is empty, and beside a card when it is not.</summary>
    private const string Threw = "▾";

    /// <summary>What a seat's face-up cards are marked with — the same token the hand uses (§3.11 A2).</summary>
    private const string FaceUpMark = DisplayTokens.FaceUp;

    /// <summary>What a seat the computer is standing in at is marked with.</summary>
    private const string StoodIn = "⟳";

    /// <summary>The seat, as the board has it.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public SeatLine Seat { get; set; } = default!;

    /// <summary>Where on the felt this seat sits, once the table has been turned to face you.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public RingSeat Seated { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whether this is your own seat, which is the one at the front.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool Yours { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The grid area this seat is drawn in — a name, never a coordinate (TableRing).</summary>
    private string Place => Seated.ToString().ToLowerInvariant();

    /// <summary>
    /// The non-colour half of every marker on this panel (§3.11 A2): a seat's state is a glyph
    /// and an edge and a tint, so that none of the three is carrying it alone.
    /// </summary>
    private string Marker => Seat switch
    {
        { Won: true } => "♛",
        { HasDeclared: true } => "✓",
        { IsTheirTurn: true } => "▶",
        _ => "·"
    };

    /// <remarks>
    /// ⚠️ <b>The state, and not the name with it.</b> The name is right there in the same
    /// paragraph and is read first, so a sentence that repeats it makes a screen reader say
    /// <em>"Cobra, Cobra is waiting"</em>. Found by reading the panel's own text back out of the
    /// browser, which is the only way any of these ever turn up.
    /// </remarks>
    private static string State(SeatLine seat) => seat switch
    {
        { Won: true } => "won the round.",
        { HasDeclared: true } => "has declared.",
        { IsTheirTurn: true } => "— it is their turn.",
        _ => "is waiting."
    };


    private string Amount => Seat.Bank switch
    {
        > 0 => $"+${Seat.Bank}",
        < 0 => $"−${-Seat.Bank}",
        _ => "$0"
    };

    private string Direction => Seat.Bank switch
    {
        > 0 => "up",
        < 0 => "down",
        _ => "level"
    };
}
